Mech v0.3 — Program Specification
===============================================================================
Corey Montella ∙ April 15, 2026
===============================================================================

MEch has finally reached a milestone that's been years in the making!

At the beginning of 2025, I described four major phases:

-(☑️) **v0.1** proof of concept system - minimum viable language implementation
-(☑️) **v0.2** data specification - formulas, defining and manipulating data
-(📍) **v0.3** program specification - functions, modules, state machines, Mika
-(☐) **v0.4** system specification - tools, distributed programs, capabilities

Today we are entering Phase 3: program specification. This is the point where Mech becomes a real programming language, with user-defined functions, explicit control flow, and the ability to model processes that have phases, all of which were missing in `v0.2`.

Today, we are announcing Mech `v0.3`, which is now Turing complete due to the following new features:

- **Enums and tagged unions** for modeling categorical data and optional values (§2)
- **User-defined functions** with pattern matching and recursion (§3)
- **State machines** for modeling ordered computation and processes with phases (§4)
- **Comprehensions** for constructing new matrices and sets from existing ones (§5)
- **Relational table joins** for powerful data manipulation (§6)
- **Mika the mech**, Mech's mascot and soon-to-be AI code assistant with `synth` and `gen` operators. (§7)

Before we get into all that, let's first look at how `v0.2` landed.

1. Version 0.2 Wrap-Up
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Version `v0.2-beta` was completed at the end of January 2025. We didn't post a formal announcement at the time since we immediately moved on to `v0.3` development.

We spent an inordinate amount of time on the `v0.2` data model, partly because in most languages the data model is an afterthought. By refusing to add more features until it became paintful, we had to stretch the data model as far as it could go. We wanted to see how expressive the language could be without Turing completeness, user defined functions, or explicit control flow, and I think the result is quite encouraging -- we were able to write programs like Kalman filters, linear regression, and even a simple physics engine without any of those features. 

The declarative data model for Mech has landed at the following set of features:

- Data types and structures: [`number`](https://docs.mech-lang.org/reference/number.html), [`string`](https://docs.mech-lang.org/reference/string.html), [`atom`](https://docs.mech-lang.org/reference/atom.html), [`map`](https://docs.mech-lang.org/reference/map.html), [`matrix`](https://docs.mech-lang.org/reference/matrix.html), [`record`](https://docs.mech-lang.org/reference/record.html), [`set`](https://docs.mech-lang.org/reference/set.html), [`table`](https://docs.mech-lang.org/reference/table.html), [`tuple`](https://docs.mech-lang.org/reference/tuple.html).
- [Expression broadcasting](https://docs.mech-lang.org/reference/broadcasting.html): applying operations over data structures without explicit loops.
- [Indexing and slicing](https://docs.mech-lang.org/reference/indexing.html): accessing elements within data structures using logical masks.
- [Operators](https://docs.mech-lang.org/reference/operators.html) and [standard library functions](https://docs.mech-lang.org/stdlib/index.html): arithmetic, logical, comparison, set, matrix etc.

You can visit any of those links to read the full documentation for that feature, with examples and explanations. The docs are still a work in progress, but they should give you a good sense of the capabilities of the `v0.2` data model.

Of course there are strict limitations on the kinds of programs you can write with that set of features, so today we are launching Mech `v0.3` with a whole new set of control flow mechanisms that make Mech more capable.

2. Enums, Tagged Unions, and Pattern Matching
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Mech now has a proper enum and tagged-union system:

```mech:enums
<color> := :red | :green | :blue
x<color> := :red
```

An enum is a defined kind with a fixed set of variants. Each variant is tagged with an atom, and can optionally carry a payload of any kind. In the example above, `color` is an enum kind with three variants: `:red`, `:green`, and `:blue`. The variable `x` has the kind `color` and is assigned the value `:red`.

The variants are themselves valid atom values, which are global constants that can be used as tags or identifiers. Because of this, `:red` is both a variant of the `color` enum and an atom value that can be used elsewhere in the program, which potentially can cause name clashes. There are tradeoffs here:

- Allowing variants to be atoms means we don't need a separate syntax for defining and using enums, which keeps the language simpler and more uniform.
- However, because atoms exist in a shared global space, reusing a value like `:blue` across different domains can introduce ambiguity or make code harder to read. To mitigate this, you can use qualified names for variants:

```mech:enums
y<color> := :color/red
```

This is similar to other langauges with atoms like Elixir.

(2.1) Tagged Unions

Tagged unions can carry payloads:

```mech:pattern-matching
<result> := :ok<u64> | :err<u64>
<option> := :some<result> | :none
x<option> := :some(:ok(42u64))
```

(2.2) Pattern Matching

The `?` operator has been added as a match operator for all values, including optional values and enum variants, arrays, tuples, and more. This construct takes the role of if statements, switch statements, and destructuring patterns in one operator. If a pattern is non-exhaustive, the compiler reports the missing cases as a hard error until they are covered. Wildcard patterns (`*`) can be usedas a catch-all for any unmatched cases.

Consider x from the previous example. We can match on it like this:

```mech:pattern-matching
code := x?
  | :some(:ok(n))  => n
  | :some(:err(e)) => e
  | :none          => 0
  | *              => 0.
```

The `?` operator is the match operator for optional values and enum variants. If a pattern is non-exhaustive, the compiler reports the missing variants — it won't let you silently drop cases.

Moreover, we can pattern match on arrays:

```mech:pattern-array
arr := [1 2 3]
code := arr?
  ├ [1 2 3] => "exact match"
  ├ [1 | tail] => "starts with 1"
  ├ [x y z] => "three elements"
  └ * => "other"
```

3. User-Defined Functions
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Functions in Mech use a pattern-matching style that should feel familiar to anyone who has used functional programming languages like Haskell or Elm.

```
factorial(x<u64>) => <u64>
  | 0 => 1
  | n => n * factorial(n - 1<u64>).

factorial(6<u64>) -- {ans}
```

The `|` arms are match guards; the first one that matches is evaluated and returned as the result of the function. You can pattern-match on multiple arguments too:

```
max(x<u64>, y<u64>) => <u64>
  ├ (0, y) => y
  ├ (x, 0) => x
  └ (x, y) => max(x - 1<u64>, y - 1<u64>) + 1<u64>.
max(4<u64>, 7<u64>) -- {ans}
```

The tree syntax (`├`, `└`) is an alternative to pipes that is more visually structured. Both styles are supported.

Functions can also broadcast automatically over matrices. If a scalar function is applied to a matrix argument, Mech applies it element-wise without any extra syntax on the call site.

```
inc(x<u64>) => <u64>
  | x => x + 1.

m := [1 2 3]
inc(m) -- [2 3 4]
```

(3.1) Tail-Call Optimization

Recursive functions that call themselves in tail position are optimized through a dedicated tail-call elimination pass. This means deeply recursive functions won't blow the stack:

```
fib(n<u64>) => <u64>
└ n => fib-acc(n, 0<u64>, 1<u64>).

fib-acc(n<u64>, a<u64>, b<u64>) => <u64>
├ (0<u64>, a, *) => a
└ (n, a, b) => fib-acc(n - 1<u64>, b, a + b).
```

The accumulator pattern above runs in constant stack space, so we can do:

```
fib(100000u64) -- {ans}
```

4. State Machines
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is the headline feature of v0.3, and it's worth explaining why we built the language around it.

In v0.2, Mech couldn't express ordered computation. It was a great data manipulation tool — you could describe datasets, run matrix operations, etc. — but you couldn't loop, couldn't conditionally branch, couldn't model anything that had *phases*. State machines fill that gap. They give Mech explicit control flow while preserving the dataflow properties that make Mech easy to reason about.

A state machine in Mech has two parts: a *specification* that declares the states and their types, and an *implementation* that defines transitions.

Here's a simple countdown:

```mech
#Counter(n<u64>) => <u64>
  ├ :Count(n<u64>)
  └ :Done(n<u64>).

#Counter(n<u64>) -> :Count(n)
  :Count(n)
    ├ n > 0u64 -> :Count(n - 1u64)
    └ n == 0u64 -> :Done(0u64)
  :Done(n) => n.

#Counter(5u64) -- 0u64
```

State arguments can be arrays too, with pattern matching in the transitions:

```mech
#VecFsm(n<u64>) => <u64>
  ├ :Scan(xs<[u64]:1,3>)
  └ :Done(out<u64>).

#VecFsm(n<u64>) -> :Scan([1u64 2u64 3u64])
  :Scan([x ... y]) -> :Done(x + y)
  :Done(out) => out.

#VecFsm(0u64) -- 4u64
```

(4.1) Bubble Sort

The best way to see state machines in action is with a real algorithm. Here is bubble sort implemented as a Mechdown document — a live, literate program that both explains and executes the algorithm.

Bubble sort works by repeatedly stepping through a list, comparing adjacent elements, and swapping them when they're out of order. We model each phase of the algorithm as a state.

```mech
#bubble-sort(arr<[u64]>) ⇒ <[u64]> :=
  ├ :Start(arr<[u64]>)
  ├ :Pass(arr<[u64]>, acc<[u64]>, swaps<u64>)
  ├ :Next(arr<[u64]>, swaps<u64>)
  ├ :Reverse(arr<[u64]>, acc<[u64]>, swaps<u64>)
  └ :Done(arr<[u64]>).

#bubble-sort(arr) → :Start(arr)
  -- Initialize first pass
  :Start(arr) → :Pass(arr, [], 0)
  -- Pass: compare adjacent elements and rebuild list in acc
  :Pass([a, b | tail], acc, swaps)
    ├ a > b → :Pass([a | tail], [b | acc], swaps + 1)
    └ *     → :Pass([b | tail], [a | acc], swaps)
  :Pass([x], acc, swaps) → :Next([x | acc], swaps)
  :Pass([], acc, swaps)  → :Next(acc, swaps)
  -- After a pass, reverse the accumulator to restore order
  :Next(arr, swaps) → :Reverse(arr, [], swaps)
  :Reverse([x | tail], acc, swaps) → :Reverse(tail, [x | acc], swaps)
  :Reverse([], acc, 0)     → :Done(acc)
  :Reverse([], acc, swaps) → :Pass(acc, [], 0)
  -- Return the sorted array
  :Done(arr) ⇒ arr.
```

The beauty here is that the state machine makes the algorithm's structure explicit. `:Pass` walks the list and collects a reversed accumulator. `:Reverse` corrects that reversal. `:Next` decides whether another pass is needed by checking the swap count. `:Done` terminates and returns the result.

To run it:

```
a<[u64]> := [4 2 1 3]
sorted := #bubble-sort(a)
-- [1 2 3 4]
```

This is the kind of program that Mech v0.2 couldn't express at all. v0.3 makes it natural.

The runtime validates state machine transition targets at compile time — if you reference a state that isn't declared in the specification, you get an error before execution. A `--trace` CLI flag is also available to print step-by-step transition traces while debugging.

5. Comprehensions
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

We've added comprehensions to the langauge, which are a way to construct new matrices and sets from existing ones using generators and optional guards. This is useful for when you want to create something like a filtered or transformed version of an existing dataset.

Matrix and set comprehensions are now fully implemented, with generators and guards:

```
[ x * x | x <- [1 2 3 4], (x % 2) == 0 ] -- [4 16]
```

Set comprehensions work the same way, and they compose well with table joins to express relational queries concisely:

```
pairs := {(1,2), (1,3), (2,8), (3,5), (3,9)}
user := 1
{fof | (u,f) <- pairs, (f,fof) <- pairs, u ⩵ user} -- {5, 9, 8}
```

This is friends-of-friends in four lines. Anyone who has written this query in SQL knows how much ceremony that normally requires.

6. Table Joins
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Speaking of relational queries: v0.3 ships the full set of relational join operators, each available as both a Unicode symbol and an ASCII word alias:

- `⋈` `table/join` — inner join
- `⟕` `table/left-outer-join`
- `⟖` `table/right-outer-join`
- `⟗` `table/full-outer-join`
- `⋉` `table/left-semi-join`
- `▷` `table/left-anti-join`

```mech
A := |id<u64> a<u64>| 
     | 1      10    | 
     | 2      20    | 
     | 3      30    |

B := |id<u64> b<u64>| 
     | 2      200   | 
     | 3      300   | 
     | 4      400   |
A ⋈ B
```

Full outer joins preserve optional kinds for columns that may be absent:

```mech
A ⟗ B
```

7. Mika the Mech
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

v0.3 also introduces **Mika** `╭⦿╮`, Mech's mascot and soon-to-be AI code assistant. She's now built into the grammar and lives in the REPL. In the current release she appears in a few spots as a compact text character — MicroMika (`╭⦿╮`) in the REPL prompt, and MiniMika (`(˙◯˙)`) for fuller expressions.

The plan is for Mika to serve as the interface for an AI assistant built into Mech in the current v0.3 release cycle. More on that soon.


8. Mechdown Figures
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

v0.3 adds a new kind of Mechdown figure: the **diagram**. Diagrams are rendered using the [Mermaid](https://mermaid-js.github.io/mermaid/#/) syntax, which supports flowcharts, sequence diagrams, class diagrams, and more.

9. Roadmap to v0.4
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

v0.3 is shipping now and development continues. The roadmap items still ahead in this release cycle include a module system, a build system, and the AI code assistant (Mika's starring role). After that, v0.4 moves into system specification — distributed programs, capability permissions, and heterogeneous computing, recovering the features that were present in v0.1 and then some.

